The third type of biomarker is that of predictive biomarkers, which are crucial for determining how well a patient might respond to specific therapies. An example of this is HER2 in breast cancer, which helps guide the use of targeted treatments like trastuzumab (Herceptin). By specifically targeting cells that overexpress the HER2 protein, these predictive biomarkers increase the likelihood of treatment success [57,58]. Finally, monitoring biomarkers are essential for tracking the effectiveness of treatment over time and detecting any potential recurrence of cancer. By utilizing these biomarkers, they allow healthcare providers to adjust therapies as needed, ensuring patients receive optimal care [59,60].
